So without further ado, let's get started.

15
00:01:36,494 --> 00:01:42,340
So before we start using Gemini, I want you to understand where this technology came from.

16
00:01:42,766 --> 00:01:48,560
So first in chapter 1, let's first understand how a computer even reads human language.

17
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:52,160
How are words converted into numbers by AI?

18
00:01:52,334 --> 00:01:53,920
How do transformers work?

19
00:01:54,158 --> 00:02:01,860
And how Gemini can process all kinds of media using multimodality and how Gemini was even created in the first place.

20
00:02:02,222 --> 00:02:07,360
So to understand this entire journey, we need to start with a very simple question.

21
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:10,880
How do you teach a computer what a word means?

22
00:02:11,246 --> 00:02:14,820
Now Javed Akhtar, who wrote Shole, has a very interesting quote.

23
00:02:15,040 --> 00:02:22,052
He says that just as bricks are not equal to buildings, words are not equal to thoughts.

24
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:26,308
You can take the same bricks and build different kinds of buildings.

25
00:02:26,926 --> 00:02:31,172
Similarly, you can take the same words and convey different thoughts.

26
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:35,620
You can use simple words to teach extremely complicated things.

27
00:02:36,142 --> 00:02:40,400
And you can use extremely complicated words to teach something very basic.

28
00:02:40,718 --> 00:02:50,436
Now this reflection matters a lot because the core thought comes from your own intuition and your ability to pay attention.

29
00:02:50,894 --> 00:02:54,020
Then you use words to convey your thoughts.

30
00:02:54,510 --> 00:03:01,188
But a computer can't really understand words because computers don't think the way humans do.

31
00:03:01,582 --> 00:03:05,700
Now imagine three words dog, puppy, and aeroplane.

32
00:03:06,160 --> 00:03:09,636
You immediately know that a dog and a puppy are related.

33
00:03:10,190 --> 00:03:13,668
But an aeroplane belongs to a completely different category.

34
00:03:14,080 --> 00:03:15,972
A computer cannot do this.

35
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:18,756
A computer only thinks in numbers.

36
00:03:19,246 --> 00:03:22,532
So how do we represent words using numbers?

37
00:03:22,990 --> 00:03:27,748
And also help a computer understand the relationship between these words.

38
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:33,530
Now the answer came from this incredible research called word2vec.

39
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,770
Now, word2vec stands for word2vector.

40
00:03:38,286 --> 00:03:42,564
You know what a word is, but now let me explain what's a vector.

41
00:03:42,880 --> 00:03:45,732
A vector is simply a list of numbers.

42
00:03:46,126 --> 00:03:48,132
Imagine three cafes on a map.

43
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:54,308
On this map, every location has a horizontal position and a vertical position.

44
00:03:54,766 --> 00:04:04,516
If I need to give the accurate location of this cafe, I can select a point on the map and put the horizontal and vertical positions in brackets.

45
00:04:04,974 --> 00:04:08,036
It's almost like a digit on a plotted graph.

46
00:04:08,334 --> 00:04:15,140
So I can say that the vector 2,3 describes cafes A location using two numbers.

47
00:04:15,630 --> 00:04:22,852
If a human being looks at this map, the human can visually understand which cafes are near to each other.

48
00:04:23,214 --> 00:04:29,828
But for a computer, these numbers tell it which cafe is nearby and which is farther away.

49
00:04:30,192 --> 00:04:40,192
This means we can describe the relationship between two things only through numbers by checking if they are close or far apart.

50
00:04:40,432 --> 00:04:42,308
Now let's take another example.

51
00:04:42,592 --> 00:04:48,512
Instead of imagining a map of cafes, let's imagine a map of words.

52
00:04:48,782 --> 00:04:52,004
Now we have tea, coffee, and helicopter.

53
00:04:52,432 --> 00:04:58,244
On this imaginary map of words, tea and coffee are very close together.

54
00:04:58,574 --> 00:05:01,092
But the helicopter is farther away.

55
00:05:01,454 --> 00:05:06,116
But how did we decide what numbers to give to each of these three words?

56
00:05:06,510 --> 00:05:07,140
Don't worry.

57
00:05:07,566 --> 00:05:09,668
I will explain that in a few minutes.

58
00:05:10,158 --> 00:05:17,092
But for now, let's assume that we have carefully calculated and assigned unique numbers to each word.

59
00:05:17,422 --> 00:05:20,676
In our cafe example, the numbers were coordinates.

60
00:05:21,198 --> 00:05:25,636
But in my map of words, they become word vectors.

61
00:05:26,032 --> 00:05:30,116
In the real world, a word vector has way more numbers.

62
00:05:30,510 --> 00:05:34,468
But who decides the numbers inside every word vector?

63
00:05:34,912 --> 00:05:36,292
This is the key question.

64
00:05:36,686 --> 00:05:44,292
The entire word-to-vec framework learns these word positions from examples of human language.

65
00:05:44,592 --> 00:05:47,428
This is where training of AI comes in.

66
00:05:47,822 --> 00:05:52,868
Imagine there's a machine, and that machine is being trained on certain material.

67
00:05:53,230 --> 00:05:56,592
That material has sentences from the English language.

68
00:05:56,910 --> 00:06:00,484
To begin with, the training material only has four sentences.

69
00:06:00,992 --> 00:06:09,284
I drank a cup of tea, I drank a cup of coffee, she ordered hot tea, she ordered hot coffee.

70
00:06:09,472 --> 00:06:13,668
The machine will understand that tea and coffee appear in similar surroundings.

71
00:06:13,872 --> 00:06:20,324
Those repeating patterns give the system evidence that the words are used in related ways.

72
00:06:20,750 --> 00:06:25,380
Now at the beginning, the model gives words small random list of numbers.

73
00:06:25,774 --> 00:06:28,164
Even their positions are not yet useful.

74
00:06:28,526 --> 00:06:36,868
The machine then tries to predict nearby numbers and learn which word vectors are far apart and which are close.

75
00:06:37,390 --> 00:06:40,772
In our brains, we usually think in two dimensions.

76
00:06:41,312 --> 00:06:45,860
But this universe of word vectors exists in many directions.

77
00:06:46,350 --> 00:06:48,292
This is what Word2Vec did.

78
00:06:48,718 --> 00:06:54,820
It helped people understand the importance of converting words into numerical representations.

79
00:06:55,246 --> 00:06:57,796
But that wasn't enough to make AI smart.

80
00:06:58,112 --> 00:07:02,116
Researchers were already working on other technologies like neural networks.

81
00:07:02,638 --> 00:07:12,068
But in 2017, Google researchers introduced a new architecture in a paper called Attention is All You Need.

82
00:07:12,398 --> 00:07:16,516
In this paper, they explained a new way to use word vectors.

83
00:07:16,974 --> 00:07:23,716
They created a new system by building on existing research to create something called the transformer.

84
00:07:24,046 --> 00:07:28,420
Now, this is the same transformer, the full form of T in ChatGPT.

85
00:07:28,672 --> 00:07:30,436
But why do we need a transformer?

86
00:07:30,798 --> 00:07:34,980
In the previous segment, I explained you how every word gets a word vector.

87
00:07:35,342 --> 00:07:43,076
But sometimes you and someone else use the same word, but you might give it a completely different meaning.

88
00:07:43,470 --> 00:07:44,996
Let's look at these two sentences.

89
00:07:45,392 --> 00:07:47,812
I deposited money at the bank.

90
00:07:48,112 --> 00:07:51,268
I watched the water flow past the bank.

91
00:07:51,632 --> 00:07:58,980
You understand bank differently in both of these sentences because surrounding words are guiding your meaning.

92
00:07:59,310 --> 00:08:02,980
Now a computer cannot do this just by looking at English words.

93
00:08:03,472 --> 00:08:09,316
A computer needs a mathematical process for doing something similar that you do naturally.

94
00:08:09,710 --> 00:08:12,868
And this is where the concept of attention comes in.

95
00:08:13,312 --> 00:08:17,552
Attention calculates which information should influence a word.

96
00:08:18,094 --> 00:08:24,452
In the first sentence, deposited and money help interpret bank as a bank which deals with money.

97
00:08:24,814 --> 00:08:29,060
In the second example, water and flow provide different clues.

98
00:08:29,550 --> 00:08:34,111
So the computer gets to know that this is not a money bank, but a river bank.

99
00:08:34,352 --> 00:08:39,172
Attention calculates scores between the words' numerical representations.

100
00:08:39,693 --> 00:08:47,140
Those scores determine how strongly each word's information contributes to the meaning of other words.

101
00:08:47,632 --> 00:08:54,820
This means that my word bank gets a number influenced by the sentence that it appears in.

102
00:08:55,182 --> 00:08:58,564
But attention as a concept already existed before this paper.

103
00:08:58,958 --> 00:09:02,244
The Transformer actually did something even better.

104
00:09:02,702 --> 00:09:10,116
Before 2017, many AI systems used a technology called recurrent neural networks or RNNs.

105
00:09:10,414 --> 00:09:12,272
I'll explain you with a simple example.

106
00:09:12,432 --> 00:09:13,792
Imagine you're reading a book.

107
00:09:13,952 --> 00:09:22,192
You read the first word and then the second word and then the third word, and you slowly build an understanding of what the author is trying to say.

108
00:09:22,446 --> 00:09:24,672
RNNs worked somewhat similarly.

109
00:09:24,846 --> 00:09:30,020
They processed a sequence step by step, passing information from one step to the next.

110
00:09:30,574 --> 00:09:31,712
But there was a problem.

111
00:09:31,952 --> 00:09:38,404
If you had a very long sentence or a document, processing information step by step can be very, very slow.

112
00:09:38,592 --> 00:09:44,752
It also becomes very difficult for the system to remember important information from earlier in the sequence.

113
00:09:44,912 --> 00:09:49,552
The researchers behind the transformer then proposed a completely different approach.

114
00:09:49,712 --> 00:09:59,172
What if, instead of relying on this step-by-step process, we allow every word to directly examine other relevant words in the sentence?

115
00:09:59,792 --> 00:10:02,340
This is called self-attention.

116
00:10:02,894 --> 00:10:05,072
Let's understand this with a simpler example.

117
00:10:05,326 --> 00:10:06,276
Look at this sentence.

118
00:10:06,734 --> 00:10:18,788
The animal didn't cross the road because it was tired Now, when I say it was tired, you immediately understand that I'm talking about the animal and not the road.

119
00:10:19,214 --> 00:10:21,444
But how does a computer figure this out?

120
00:10:21,872 --> 00:10:25,860
The attention calculates which relationships are relevant.

121
00:10:26,286 --> 00:10:36,036
It predicts that the word it is probably connected to the animal and not the road because it has seen similar patterns in its pre-training phase.

122
00:10:36,558 --> 00:10:39,492
And here is where things get more interesting.

123
00:10:39,854 --> 00:10:51,396
The transformer can calculate these relationships across many positions in a sentence in parallel instead of depending on RNNs step by step processing.

124
00:10:51,758 --> 00:11:00,964
Now, this made the architecture much better suited for training on enormous amounts of text using modern computer hardware and GPUs.

125
00:11:01,358 --> 00:11:03,652
But the researchers faced another problem.

126
00:11:04,032 --> 00:11:08,592
If words are processed in parallel, how does the computer know their order?

127
00:11:08,752 --> 00:11:10,116
Let's take two sentences.

128
00:11:10,592 --> 00:11:15,236
The dog chased the boy and the boy chased the dog.

129
00:11:15,566 --> 00:11:21,732
Both sentences contain almost the same words, but their meanings are completely different.

130
00:11:22,030 --> 00:11:27,792
This is why the original transformer also introduced positional information.

131
00:11:28,046 --> 00:11:36,132
Along with the numerical representation of every word, the system receives information about where that word appears in the sentence.

132
00:11:36,622 --> 00:11:40,548
This helps the transformer distinguish between word orders.

133
00:11:40,942 --> 00:11:42,820
Now there's one more important concept.

134
00:11:43,278 --> 00:11:45,380
It's called multi head attention.

135
00:11:45,742 --> 00:11:48,032
Again, I'll explain with a simple example.

136
00:11:48,272 --> 00:11:51,204
Imagine you're reading an important business contract.

137
00:11:51,726 --> 00:12:02,820
Now, you as a business owner might focus on the payment terms, while your lawyer might focus on the legal obligations, while your accountant focuses on the tax implications.

138
00:12:03,104 --> 00:12:08,996
All three of you are reading the same document, but you're looking for different kinds of relationships.

139
00:12:09,264 --> 00:12:12,452
Multi-head attention works on somewhat similar principle.

140
00:12:12,704 --> 00:12:19,140
Instead of calculating just one set of attention relationships, the transformer calculates multiple sets.

141
00:12:19,534 --> 00:12:23,972
Different attention heads can learn to focus on different language patterns.

142
00:12:24,462 --> 00:12:29,284
But so far, we've only discussed how a transformer processes information.

143
00:12:29,744 --> 00:12:32,292
How does it actually generate an answer?

144
00:12:32,686 --> 00:12:40,932
And this brings us to two important terms from the original transformer architecture, the encoder and the decoder.

145
00:12:41,104 --> 00:12:45,984
Imagine you have hired two people to translate an English document into French.

146
00:12:46,254 --> 00:12:52,388
The first person's job is to read the English document and understand the information it contains.

147
00:12:52,944 --> 00:12:58,532
The second person's job is to use that information to write the document in French.

148
00:12:58,830 --> 00:13:05,444
Now, this example can help you imagine how the encoder and the decoder work together.

149
00:13:05,838 --> 00:13:14,852
In the original 2017 transformer, the encoder and the decoder worked together for tasks like language translation.

150
00:13:15,310 --> 00:13:21,424
However, modern AI models don't necessarily use both paths in exactly the same way.

151
00:13:21,678 --> 00:13:25,744
In upcoming sessions, I'll keep explaining you the modern architectures as well.

152
00:13:25,934 --> 00:13:29,572
But there's another very interesting problem that scientists faced.

153
00:13:29,824 --> 00:13:33,104
Everything we've discussed so far has been about words.

154
00:13:33,326 --> 00:13:38,052
What happens if I give AI an image or a video or an audio recording?

155
00:13:38,478 --> 00:13:42,596
How does it connect all that information with human language?

156
00:13:42,864 --> 00:13:48,144
This is where we finally begin to understand why Google built Gemini.

157
00:13:48,654 --> 00:13:55,556
Because Gemini from day one was designed to work with multiple types of information and not just text.

158
00:13:56,078 --> 00:13:59,236
And by the way, even OpenAI does the same.

159
00:13:59,630 --> 00:14:01,412
Claude is still restrictive.

160
00:14:01,806 --> 00:14:07,104
And this brings to an entirely new concept called Multi-model AI.

161
00:14:07,264 --> 00:14:10,436
Let's first understand what multimodality actually means.

162
00:14:10,704 --> 00:14:13,156
Imagine you're sitting in a meeting with a client.

163
00:14:13,486 --> 00:14:15,748
Your client is explaining a business problem.

164
00:14:16,064 --> 00:14:23,492
While they're speaking, They're also showing you a presentation and he's pointing towards certain numbers and explaining why their sales have dropped.

165
00:14:23,664 --> 00:14:33,344
Now, as a human being, you can listen to what your client is saying, also read the information on slides, observe what they are pointing towards, everything happening at the same time.

166
00:14:33,582 --> 00:14:39,556
So your brain combines all of this information, all of this context to understand the situation.

167
00:14:39,824 --> 00:14:42,372
But think what is even happening here.

168
00:14:42,606 --> 00:14:52,740
You're not just understanding words, you're understanding images, sounds, numbers, expressions, and the relationship between all of this information.

169
00:14:53,102 --> 00:15:00,420
Now these are different types of information, and in the world of artificial intelligence, we call them modalities.

170
00:15:00,942 --> 00:15:08,580
So text is one modality, images are another modality, audio and video are also separate modalities.

171
00:15:08,814 --> 00:15:16,836
When an AI system can process and combine multiple types of information, we call it a multi-model AI system.

172
00:15:17,230 --> 00:15:18,944
Now there's a very interesting problem here.

173
00:15:19,214 --> 00:15:25,348
Earlier I explained how we can represent words using vectors, which are basically a list of numbers.

174
00:15:25,806 --> 00:15:28,516
But how do you convert images into numbers?

175
00:15:29,038 --> 00:15:31,556
So let's imagine a photograph of a dog.

176
00:15:31,886 --> 00:15:36,624
When you look at a photograph, you immediately understand that you're looking at a dog.

177
00:15:37,134 --> 00:15:41,124
But a computer doesn't see a photograph the way humans do.

178
00:15:41,424 --> 00:15:46,436
A digital image is made up of these tiny, tiny elements called pixels.

179
00:15:46,864 --> 00:15:51,332
Each pixel has numerical values representing its color.

180
00:15:51,664 --> 00:15:57,348
And all of these colors can be explained in RGB values.

181
00:15:57,806 --> 00:16:02,660
So literally any pixel in the world is a combination of these RGB digits.

182
00:16:03,118 --> 00:16:08,196
And if you combine millions of these pixels, you get the photograph that you're looking at.

183
00:16:08,686 --> 00:16:15,908
So technically speaking, an image is already represented using numbers in every computer you have ever used.

184
00:16:16,302 --> 00:16:22,304
But just having those numbers doesn't mean that a computer can understand that the photograph contains a dog.

185
00:16:22,464 --> 00:16:32,676
The AI model has to learn how those visual patterns and how those numbers in pixels relate to certain objects or shapes or colors and other things.

186
00:16:33,134 --> 00:16:36,164
And this is where neural networks come in.

187
00:16:36,462 --> 00:16:39,908
And they're actually inspired by the neurons in the human brain.

188
00:16:40,334 --> 00:16:49,984
So just as language models learn relationships between words, image processing networks can learn relationships between different parts of an image.

189
00:16:50,254 --> 00:17:00,004
They can learn that certain patterns of numbers represent the eyes and ears and faces and eventually a lot more complicated visual relationships.

190
00:17:00,624 --> 00:17:02,180
But what about audio?

191
00:17:02,478 --> 00:17:04,996
How can audio be converted into numbers?

192
00:17:05,198 --> 00:17:12,464
When you speak into a microphone, just like I am speaking into this mic, your voice creates vibrations in the air.

193
00:17:12,704 --> 00:17:21,284
The microphone converts those vibrations into an electrical signal, which can then be recorded digitally as numbers.

194
00:17:21,710 --> 00:17:29,220
And can you imagine all of this is just millions of electrons flowing at the speed of light?

195
00:17:29,550 --> 00:17:31,556
It's actually magical if you think about it.

196
00:17:31,904 --> 00:17:38,692
Even this video that you're watching right now, everything that you see is being dumbed down to just numbers.

197
00:17:39,086 --> 00:17:39,844
And you know what?

198
00:17:40,384 --> 00:17:45,824
Even those numbers are dumbed down to only zeros and ones.

199
00:17:46,304 --> 00:17:53,344
Every single number is being only read as zeros and ones by the computer.

200
00:17:53,710 --> 00:17:57,220
But it's happening so quickly that it's almost magical.

201
00:17:57,550 --> 00:18:06,052
Now AI systems can process these numerical signals to learn patterns related to speech and sounds and any kind of information.

202
00:18:06,464 --> 00:18:15,812
Video is even more interesting because it contains images that change over time at say 30 frames per second, and they're often accompanied by audio.

203
00:18:16,206 --> 00:18:23,876
So whether you're dealing with text, images, audio, or video, computers need to represent all of this information numerically.

204
00:18:24,238 --> 00:18:27,108
And almost all leading models now are multi-model.

205
00:18:27,470 --> 00:18:39,780
But one advantage that makes Google Gemini extremely useful compared to other alternatives is that it already has a large ecosystem of products that businesses use every single day.

206
00:18:40,174 --> 00:18:44,100
And when it comes to research, I don't think Gemini has any competition.

207
00:18:44,494 --> 00:18:49,700
Plus, it's extremely affordable, and on a lot of plans, you also get Gemini for free.

208
00:18:49,904 --> 00:18:53,184
They also have tons of discounts on student IDs as well.

209
00:18:53,422 --> 00:18:55,984
Now, Gemini is actually not the actual AI.

210
00:18:56,144 --> 00:19:03,396
Gemini is the platform, it's the system that allows you to access Google's entire family of models.

211
00:19:03,982 --> 00:19:07,344
And each model is designed for a different kind of work.

212
00:19:07,758 --> 00:19:11,972
So now that we have finished chapter 1, it's time to move on to the next chapter.

213
00:19:12,366 --> 00:19:15,044
Well, I will explain how Gemini actually works.

214
00:19:15,598 --> 00:19:18,624
But first, let's quickly revise everything we've learned so far.

215
00:19:18,990 --> 00:19:27,716
So I first explained you how a computer reads a word, how transformers use encoders and decoders, and how important multimodality is.

216
00:19:28,142 --> 00:19:31,504
But now that this is clear, let's move on to chapter number two.

217
00:19:32,046 --> 00:19:39,172
Some Gemini models are designed for faster and more affordable tasks because anytime you pay to Gemini, you get certain credits.

